# PCAP capture settings: where the sensor listens and how captures rotate.
# Append to <malcolm>/config/pcap-capture.env.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Malcolm's live capture is what feeds both the NDR pipeline's watch directory
# and Arkime's sessions. Its defaults are a loopback interface and an unfiltered
# capture, which is a development configuration, not a sensor.
#
# Placement
# ---------
# The OT visibility standard is a passive tap or a SPAN/mirror port, one-way, so
# the sensor can never touch the process network. Point PCAP_IFACE at the
# mirrored interface:
#
#   - SPAN/mirror port  -> PCAP_IFACE=eth1 (the port the switch mirrors to)
#   - network TAP       -> PCAP_IFACE=eth1 (the monitor-side TAP interface)
#   - span aggregation  -> PCAP_IFACE=eth1,eth2 (comma-separated)
#
# Keep the capture one-way: never put the sensor interface in the same bridge as
# the control traffic, and never enable anything that transmits on it.
#
# Install
# -------
#   cat pcap-capture.env.fragment >> <malcolm>/config/pcap-capture.env
#   cd <malcolm> && docker compose up -d --force-recreate pcap-capture

# The interface Malcolm captures on. Do not leave this at the default.
PCAP_IFACE=lo

# Restrict the capture to OT traffic so the sensor is not filling disks with
# everything else. Adjust to the plant's addressing and the protocols in
# ot-detection-engineering.
#   Modbus 502, DNP3 20000, OPC UA 4840, S7comm 102
# PCAP_FILTER=tcp port 502 or tcp port 20000 or tcp port 4840 or tcp port 102

# Rotation: rotated captures land in <malcolm>/pcap and are exactly what the NDR
# pipeline's watch mode consumes. Smaller windows mean lower latency and more
# files to profile.
PCAP_ROTATE_MINUTES=10
PCAP_ROTATE_MEGABYTES=4096

# Analyse rotated files or the interface directly. Malcolm chooses between them
# with SURICATA_LIVE_CAPTURE/ZEEK_LIVE_CAPTURE (analyse the interface) and
# SURICATA_ROTATED_PCAP/ZEEK_ROTATED_PCAP (analyse the rotated files, set in the
# *-offline.env run profiles). This pipeline needs the rotated files to exist in
# <malcolm>/pcap, so check which pair is active in your profile:
#
#   grep -r ROTATED_PCAP LIVE_CAPTURE <malcolm>/config/*.env
#
# Whichever pair is active, confirm files are appearing:
#
#   ls -l <malcolm>/pcap
